ADOPT: one new file, `analyzers/AgentGuard.Analyzers/WrittenNameScanner.cs`, `internal static class WrittenNameScanner` in `namespace AgentGuard.Analyzers` — the written-name lens, sibling to `MemberUseScanner` (member-use lens) and the contracted `DeclaredTypeScanner` (carried-type lens). The name and its responsibilities go to Tim for approval before RULE-PHASE builds against it; the contract reserved exactly this ("DESIGN names that second file and brings its name and responsibilities for approval"), so until he says yes it is an open item, not a decision.

RESPONSIBILITIES — one judgment only: "this node writes a name, and here is the symbol it binds to." It takes no position on whether that symbol is permitted anywhere. Every exemption lives in the consuming rule's own inspector.

SURFACE (mirrors `MemberUseScanner.cs:43` / `:61` exactly, so a rule composing both lenses writes structurally identical setup for each):

```csharp
internal static class WrittenNameScanner
{
    // The two concrete SimpleNameSyntax kinds. Exhaustive by construction: every written name bottoms out at one.
    private static readonly ImmutableArray<SyntaxKind> NameKinds =
        ImmutableArray.Create(SyntaxKind.IdentifierName, SyntaxKind.GenericName);

    internal static void Register(
        CompilationStartAnalysisContext context,
        Action<SyntaxNodeAnalysisContext, ISymbol> inspect)
    {
        context.RegisterSyntaxNodeAction(nodeContext => Dispatch(nodeContext, inspect), NameKinds);
    }

    internal static void RegisterForAssembly(
        CompilationStartAnalysisContext context,
        string assemblyName,
        Action<SyntaxNodeAnalysisContext, ISymbol> inspect)
    {
        if (!OwnerClass.InAssembly(assemblyName)(context.Compilation))
        {
            return;
        }

        Register(context, inspect);
    }

    private static void Dispatch(SyntaxNodeAnalysisContext context, Action<SyntaxNodeAnalysisContext, ISymbol> inspect)
    {
        ISymbol? symbol = context.SemanticModel.GetSymbolInfo(context.Node, context.CancellationToken).Symbol;
        if (symbol is not null)
        {
            inspect(context, symbol);
        }
    }
}
```

Every API here is verified by reflection against Roslyn 4.14.0: `CompilationStartAnalysisContext.RegisterSyntaxNodeAction` has the `ImmutableArray<TLanguageKindEnum>` overload; `SyntaxNodeAnalysisContext` exposes `Node`, `ContainingSymbol` (typed `ISymbol`), `SemanticModel`, `CancellationToken`. `ContainingSymbol` being present is what lets the already-approved method-level `CompositionPoint` caller check plug in with zero adaptation.

FOUR DESIGN POINTS, each settled by probe evidence rather than by grammar recall:

1. Two kinds, no QualifiedName, no AliasQualifiedName, no per-position registration. Every contract-named position bottoms out at a leaf IdentifierName or GenericName that `RegisterSyntaxNodeAction` visits at any depth. Probe-confirmed for all of: `typeof`, `nameof` both bare and dotted, cast, `as`, `is`-pattern, `case`-pattern, generic type argument, generic constraint, base and interface list, attribute argument, using-alias use, using-alias target, array element, pointer element, `global::`-qualified receiver, nested type, and every declaration position. There is no eleventh position to forget because the set was never an enumeration — which is precisely the failure AG0007 (`ContractConcreteTypeMustNotBeCastToAnalyzer.cs:49-52`, four hand-picked kinds) exhibits and this design must not repeat.

2. `GenericName` is load-bearing, not optional. `GenericNameSyntax` holds its identifier as a TOKEN, not as a child `IdentifierNameSyntax` — the probe's only hit for `GuardedG<int>` was the GenericName node itself. Dropping that kind loses every reference to a guarded type that is itself generic, in every position. A required fixture (rule R5) pins it.

3. The callback hands back `ISymbol`, un-narrowed, and takes no third parameter. `ISymbol` is required: an attribute's own name node resolves to the attribute's constructor `IMethodSymbol`, so an `INamedTypeSymbol` callback misses an internal Engine type used as an attribute; and AG0041's member half ("an internal Engine member named anywhere other than the approved call") needs `IMethodSymbol`/`IPropertySymbol`/`IFieldSymbol` anyway — the probe confirms all of them resolve through this one registration, including positions with no `IOperation` at all. No `SimpleNameSyntax` parameter: `context.Node` already carries it, and `MemberUseScanner`'s precedent passes back only what resolving ADDED. No `Describe` helper: `MemberUseScanner.Describe` exists only because that scanner splits one use into member + declaring type; here `symbol.ToDisplayString()` is the whole need, so a wrapper would be a speculative hatch.

4. Syntax-based, not operation-based. A generic constraint, a base-list entry, and an attribute-argument type are declaration-time constructs with no `IOperation` representation, so no number of registered operation kinds reaches them. That is why this cannot be a second `MemberUseScanner` configuration.

WHAT THE SCANNER DOES NOT DO — `TypeTree.Any` stays out of it, and stays IN each consuming rule's leaf test. The contract's reuse ledger already rules this ("reuse `analyzers/AgentGuard.Analyzers/TypeTree.cs:25` … AG0041 passes its own leaf test into it"; and the same for the AG0023/AG0029 halves). It is not redundant with leaf registration, and simplest's claim that it would be "inert" is wrong in one real case: a `var` local, or a generic name with an inferred argument, resolves to a composite symbol such as `List<Guarded>` whose nested guarded type is written nowhere — only the symbol walk reaches it. Scanner = resolve one node. Consuming predicate = `TypeTree.Any(resolvedType, leafTest)`. That split satisfies the contract's "Each lens tests every type reachable through generic arguments, array elements, and pointer targets."

WIRING (RULE-PHASE's work, stated so the surface is proven usable):
- AG0041 `EngineInternalsOneDoorAnalyzer`: `WrittenNameScanner.RegisterForAssembly(context, CliAssembly.CompiledName, Inspect)` and again with `TestAssembly.TestHelpersName` — the same two-call shape AG0023/AG0029 already use for their two gates. Predicate: declared in `AgentGuard.Engine` AND `DeclaredAccessibility == Accessibility.Internal` (public Engine APIs must stay unreported), run through `TypeTree.Any` for the nested case, with member symbols judged by `ContainingType`.
- AG0023 `OneDoorIntoCrossPlatformAnalyzer` and AG0029 `OneDoorIntoPerOsAnalyzer`: one `WrittenNameScanner.RegisterForAssembly(context, EngineAssembly.Name, Inspect)` each, on the ENGINE gate only, beside their existing `MemberUseScanner` call. Each reuses its existing assembly-scope test and `WellKnownType.IsInAssembly` for its own door, combined with the new `CompositionPoint` method-level caller check.

THE EXEMPTION COVERS TWO NODES, NOT ONE. For the one approved call `SystemServices.Create()`, the probe shows the written-name lens fires TWICE — once on the `SystemServices` receiver (NamedType) and once on `Create` (Method). AG0041 must exempt both, or the approved call is still reported by the member half. None of the three candidates stated this. The live call sites are safe as written: `Program.cs:62` is `new Program(args).Run(SystemServices.Create())`, `SystemServicesBuilder.cs:77` is `real: SystemServices.Create()`, and `SystemServicesBuilder.cs:311` declares `ISystemServices services = …` (the public Abstractions interface, never the internal type) — no `var` and no internal declared type at any of the three.

PRODUCTION IMPACT OF THE NEW ENGINE GATES IS ZERO TODAY. `grep -rn "AgentGuard\.CrossPlatform|AgentGuard\.Boundaries|CrossPlatformAdapters|PlatformServices|EnvironmentAdapter|ConsoleAdapter|Ed25519SignatureService|BuildInfoReader" src/AgentGuard.Engine --include="*.cs"` returns nothing, so after the move the only Engine file naming a guarded type is the relocated `SystemServices.cs`, and both of its references sit inside `Create()` and are exempt.

